#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060195 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + t & 2t \\\\ -3 & 1 - t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A) = 10$, what is the value of $t$?" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\det(A)&=\\begin{vmatrix}1+t & 2t \\\\ -3 & 1-t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=(1+t)(1-t)-(2t)(-3) \\\\\n&=1-t^2+6t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=10$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1-t^2+6t&=10 \\\\\nt^2-6t+9&=0 \\\\\n(t-3)^2&=0 \\\\\nt&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060226 :question/problem "What is $\\cos θ + \\frac{\\sin^{2} θ}{\\cos θ}$?" :question/worked-solution "Use a common denominator and the Pythagorean identity:\n$$\\cos\\theta+\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}=\\sec\\theta.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060252 :question/problem "The interval $y ∈ [- 5, 2\\sqrt{2})$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "The square bracket includes $-5$ and the parenthesis excludes $2\\sqrt2$. Therefore $-5\\le y<2\\sqrt2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060264 :question/problem "![](images/a2/a2cc171a455a193deab3555b823b715d887d372c8c9b57933f525edcfc99e150.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The open circle at $-1$ excludes that endpoint, and the filled circle at $2$ includes that endpoint. The segment represents $x\\in(-1,2]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060267 :question/problem "![](images/a2/a2e3d6a49f516b3037e0be9cb74312a59d2a924a6ec768a72660485951bb0302.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Both endpoint circles, at $1$ and $4$, are open. The segment represents $x\\in(1,4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060278 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}$." :question/worked-solution "The matrix is diagonal, so its determinant is the product of its diagonal entries:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot7\\cdot(-3)=-42.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060310 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x ∈ (3, 4)$?" :question/worked-solution "Both parentheses exclude their endpoints. Therefore $x\\in(3,4)$ means $3<x<4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060311 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x ∈ [- 2, 1]$?" :question/worked-solution "Both square brackets include their endpoints. Therefore $x\\in[-2,1]$ means $-2\\le x\\le1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060343 :question/problem "What is $\\cos^{2} θ(1 + \\frac{\\sin^{2} θ}{\\cos^{2} θ})$?" :question/worked-solution "Where the original denominator is nonzero,\n$$\\cos^2\\theta\\left(1+\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos^2\\theta}\\right)=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060344 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\cos^{3} θ + \\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ$." :question/worked-solution "Factor out $\\cos\\theta$:\n$$\\cos^3\\theta+\\cos\\theta\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos\\theta(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=\\cos\\theta.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060345 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\cos^{2} θ\\sin^{2} θ + \\sin^{4} θ}{\\cos θ\\sin θ}$." :question/worked-solution "On the domain of the original fraction,\n$$\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta\\sin^2\\theta+\\sin^4\\theta}{\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta}=\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}{\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta}=\\tan\\theta.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060346 :question/problem "What is $\\frac{\\sin^{2} x}{\\sin^{3} x + \\sin x\\cos^{2} x}$?" :question/worked-solution "For the original expression, $\\sin x\\ne0$. Factoring its denominator gives\n$$\\frac{\\sin^2x}{\\sin^3x+\\sin x\\cos^2x}=\\frac{\\sin^2x}{\\sin x(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x)}=\\sin x.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060349 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin^{2} x\\cos^{3} x + \\sin^{4} x\\cos x$." :question/worked-solution "Factor and apply the Pythagorean identity:\n$$\\sin^2x\\cos^3x+\\sin^4x\\cos x=\\sin^2x\\cos x(\\cos^2x+\\sin^2x)=\\sin^2x\\cos x.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060392 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ if $\\mathbf{a}=7\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$." :question/worked-solution "Collect the corresponding components:\n$$(7\\mathbf i-6\\mathbf j)+(3\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j)=10\\mathbf i-4\\mathbf j.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060393 :question/problem "Find $2\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ if $\\mathbf{a}=4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=6\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}$." :question/worked-solution "Distribute the scalar and collect components:\n$$2(4\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j)+(6\\mathbf i-4\\mathbf j)=14\\mathbf i.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060446 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ x & 2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & x + 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis $8$, find all the possible values of $x$.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The determinant is $x^{2} + 10 x + 24$. Setting it equal to $8$ gives $\\left\\{-8, -2\\right\\}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060447 :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2k+1 & 3 & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2k+1 & 3 & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}2k+1 & k \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-4\\begin{vmatrix}2k+1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(3-0)-(2k+1-k)-4(0-3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(k+1)-4\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6-k-1+12 \\\\  & =17-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17-k & =0 \\\\ k & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $k=17.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060454 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $F$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-1(24-30)+2(21-24) \\\\  & =0-1\\cdot (-6)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(F)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements $det(F)=0$ and “$F$ is a singular matrix” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060455 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}$." :question/worked-solution "Expanding across the first row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0&-1&3\\\\2&0&1\\\\7&-1&0\\end{vmatrix}&=0-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}2&1\\\\7&0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}2&0\\\\7&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=(-7)+3(-2)\\\\&=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060456 :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 & 4k \\\\ 0 & 9 & 5k \\\\ 0 & 11 & 6k\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular." :question/worked-solution "The determinant is $0$. Setting it equal to $0$ gives $\\mathbb{R}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064038 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=2$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} + C = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\ &= {x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064039 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of  $\\sqrt x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the square root as a rational power, $\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064040 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ and that $F'(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if $F'(x) = f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac 1 {x^3}= x^{-3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064068 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 x \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064069 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int _{1}^2\\dfrac 1 {x^3}\\, \\textrm d x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{-2}-{1}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064070 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_1^4\\sqrt x\\, \\textrm d x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\sqrt{64}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064099 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv' \\, \\textrm{d}x= uv - \\int vu' \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle  \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle  \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{7x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}-\\int \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}\\int {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{7x}}{7}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{49}{e}^{7x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064100 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int x \\cos{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x \\cos{2x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064117 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/5} x\\sin(5x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\int (-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+{\\frac{1}{5}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\right)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)]+[\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\pi }{25}\\cdot (-1)+0]+[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064484 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+3\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x+3)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(x+3)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064508 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 6x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{4x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6x\\cdot {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}-\\int \\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3x{e}^{4x}}{2}-\\frac{3{e}^{4x}}{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064509 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064554 :question/problem "Calculate the integral.\n\n$$\n\\int x\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int x\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C\\right)=x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064556 :question/problem "Calculate the integral.\n\n$$\n\\int x^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int x^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{x^{5}}{5} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}+C\\right)=x^{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064557 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{x^{2}}$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int x^{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{3x^{5/3}}{5} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3 x^{\\frac{5}{3}}}{5}+C\\right)=x^{\\frac{2}{3}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064634 :question/problem "Calculate\n\n$$\n\\int x2^{x}dx\n$$." :question/worked-solution "Use integration by parts with $u=x$ and $\\mathrm dv=2^x\\,\\mathrm dx$. Then $\\mathrm du=\\mathrm dx$ and $v=2^x/\\ln2$, so\n$$\n\\int x2^x\\,\\mathrm dx=\\frac{x2^x}{\\ln2}-\\frac1{\\ln2}\\int 2^x\\,\\mathrm dx\n=\\frac{2^x}{\\ln2}\\left(x-\\frac1{\\ln2}\\right)+C.\n$$\nDifferentiating the result gives $x2^x$, which verifies the antiderivative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064712 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}x^{3}dx\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "An antiderivative of $x^3$ is $x^4/4$. Thus\n$$\\int_{-2}^{2}x^3\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^4}{4}\\right]_{-2}^{2}=4-4=0.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064713 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $x^{1/3}$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{3x^{4/3}}{4} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3 x^{\\frac{4}{3}}}{4}+C\\right)=\\sqrt[3]{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064714 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{x^{5}}dx.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x\\ge0$ on the interval, $\\sqrt{x^5}=x^{5/2}$. Hence\n$$\\int_0^4\\sqrt{x^5}\\,dx=\\left[\\frac27x^{7/2}\\right]_0^4=\\frac27(128)=\\frac{256}{7}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064716 :question/problem "Evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1}{|}_{-5}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-5}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-5)^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{125}{3} \\\\  & =63.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
